{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0504"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge technology/computing","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/technology__computing.g2.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",106],["-","Five"],["+","Six"],["=",541],["-","five."],["+","six."],["=",315],["+","The blog took the same road from the other direction. In December 1997 the American writer Jorn Barger called his website, a daily log of the links he found while surfing, a weblog – a log of the web. In 1999 the designer Peter Merholz jokingly split the word into \"we blog\" in the sidebar of his own site, and the clipped form stuck: the weblog (blog) became so common that \"to blog\" and \"blogger\" followed, and today the short form has all but replaced the original.\n\n"],["=",127],["+","\n| Weblog (blog) | web and log | 1997, Jorn Barger |"]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",94],["-","Fünf"],["+","Sechs"],["=",493],["-","fünf."],["+","sechs."],["=",311],["+","Das Blog ging denselben Weg aus der anderen Richtung. Im Dezember 1997 nannte der amerikanische Autor Jorn Barger seine Website, ein tägliches Protokoll der Links, die er beim Surfen gefunden hatte, ein Weblog – ein Logbuch des Webs. 1999 zerlegte der Designer Peter Merholz das Wort in der Seitenleiste seiner eigenen Website scherzhaft in „we blog“, und die Kurzform setzte sich durch: Das Weblog (Blog) wurde so alltäglich, dass „bloggen“ und „Blogger“ folgten, und heute hat die Kurzform das Original fast verdrängt.\n\n"],["=",127],["+","\n| Weblog (Blog) | Web und Log | 1997, Jorn Barger |"]]}]},
